# Small-Team Workflow — local SQLite + BYO Postgres sync hub

> **Project:** {{ .ProjectPrefix }}
> **Generated by:** mtix {{ .Version }}
> **Workflow audience:** 2-10 trusted developers sharing one Postgres
> sync hub (Supabase, Neon, RDS, or self-hosted). Suitable for small
> startups and OSS team adoption.

> **Trust banner.** The sync hub is a replication mechanism, not a
> tenancy boundary. Anyone with the hub DSN can read every event ever
> pushed. Treat the DSN like a production database credential.

In this workflow every developer's CLI keeps its **own local SQLite**
as the canonical store (`.mtix/data/mtix.db`). Mutations emit events
to a local queue; `mtix sync push` ships the queue to the BYO Postgres
hub; `mtix sync pull` replays others' events into the local store.
`.mtix/tasks.json` remains the plain-text git-tracked snapshot.

---

## Setup steps

### 1. Provision a Postgres hub

Managed (Supabase, Neon, RDS) or self-hosted. Make sure TLS with a
trusted CA is enabled. Note the host, port, database name.

### 2. Create a least-privilege role for mtix

Run as a PG superuser once during provisioning. `mtix_sync` runs
`mtix sync init`, so it creates and owns the sync tables; `sync_projects`
is written only by init, as the owner. A role that syncs without owning
the tables needs this least-privilege list:

- USAGE on the schema;
- SELECT on `sync_events`, `sync_hub_state`, `sync_node_collisions` and
  `sync_project_clients`;
- INSERT on `sync_events`, `sync_conflicts` and `sync_project_clients`;
- UPDATE on `sync_project_clients`;
- USAGE on the sequence `sync_conflicts_conflict_id_seq`;
- EXECUTE on the function `record_restore_collision`;
- UPDATE on `sync_node_collisions`, only for a role that runs
  `mtix sync collisions resolve`;
- SELECT on `node_renumber_remaps`, only for a role that runs
  `mtix sync migrate` on a hub without a valid node-number registry
  index;
- INSERT on `node_renumber_remaps`, only for a role that runs
  `mtix sync migrate --yes` on a hub without a valid node-number registry
  index.

On a hub without a valid node-number registry index, `mtix sync migrate
--yes` also builds that index when the version gate is open, that is, when
the project has at least one active client and every active client runs a
remap-aware mtix version. It first records the duplicate creates of every
project on the hub, and the index leaves those creates out, so they stay in
the event log unchanged and the build succeeds. It drops an index that is
not valid or not ready and builds it again. While the gate is closed, it
leaves the index for a later run. A hub with more duplicate creates than
the index can leave out is refused before the build, with the count and the
limit. Only the table owner can build the index.

`mtix sync doctor` fails its `schema current` check while the registry
index is not valid or not ready, and `mtix sync init` warns about such an
index; both print the fix: as the table owner, run `mtix sync migrate
--yes` while the version gate is open. On a hub without the index whose
projects hold duplicate creates, `mtix sync init` refuses and names the
same fix; run `mtix sync init` again after it.

The hub stamps every event with its own restore epoch: a trigger on
`sync_events` sets it from `sync_hub_state` on every insert. A syncing
role records restore collisions only through the hub function
`record_restore_collision`, which runs as the table owner and records a
collision only when the hub's own data shows an earlier-epoch create
holding the number, so the role needs EXECUTE on that function and no
INSERT on `sync_node_collisions`.

When `mtix sync init` adds this function to an existing hub, the table
owner then grants EXECUTE on it to each syncing role, and revokes the
privileges the list no longer names:

```sql
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION record_restore_collision TO <role>;
-- once every syncing client is upgraded:
REVOKE INSERT ON sync_node_collisions FROM <role>;
REVOKE USAGE ON SEQUENCE sync_node_collisions_collision_id_seq FROM <role>;
```

Upgrade every syncing client first, then run the REVOKE statements; if the
REVOKE comes first, an older client's push that meets a restore collision
fails until that client upgrades.

`mtix sync doctor`, run with a syncing role's DSN, names that role in its
`schema current` check when it cannot execute the function, with the
exact GRANT to run, and when it can write collision rows, with the exact
REVOKE the table owner runs for a plain grant the owner made and every
other path by name, which a role administrator removes (see "Hub health
checks" in the user manual). The check skips the table owner, roles that inherit it, and superusers.

A dump holds no privileges: after restoring the hub and running
`mtix sync init`, grant each syncing role this list again, EXECUTE on
`record_restore_collision` included, then run `mtix sync doctor` with a
syncing role's DSN.

A syncing role set up with the least-privilege list holds no UPDATE on
`sync_hub_state`, so it cannot run `mtix sync mark-restored`, which runs
as the table owner.

A role that runs `mtix sync backup` also needs SELECT on every sync table
and on the sequences `audit_log_audit_id_seq`,
`sync_conflicts_conflict_id_seq` and `sync_node_collisions_collision_id_seq`;
otherwise run the backup as the table owner.

CREATE on the schema is needed whenever `mtix sync init` runs: the first
run, after an upgrade that adds a migration, and in the restore runbook.

These statements are for PostgreSQL 15 and later. The superuser that runs
them owns the hub database, which grants CREATE on schema `public` only to
the roles named here; `mtix_sync` owns only the objects `mtix sync init`
creates, and its privileges on schema `public` are the ones granted here.

```sql
CREATE ROLE mtix_sync LOGIN PASSWORD 'set-a-strong-one';
CREATE DATABASE mtix_hub;
\c mtix_hub
REVOKE CREATE ON SCHEMA public FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE mtix_hub TO mtix_sync;
GRANT USAGE, CREATE ON SCHEMA public TO mtix_sync;
```

Between runs of `mtix sync init` you may drop schema-level CREATE, and
grant it again before the next run:

```sql
REVOKE CREATE ON SCHEMA public FROM mtix_sync;
-- before the next mtix sync init:
GRANT CREATE ON SCHEMA public TO mtix_sync;
```

### 3. Store the DSN

Two sources, in order of preference. Positional DSN arguments are no
longer accepted; set `MTIX_SYNC_DSN` or `.mtix/secrets`.

```bash
# Preferred (production): env var
export MTIX_SYNC_DSN="postgresql://mtix_sync@hub.example.com:5432/mtix_hub?sslmode=verify-full"

# Alternative (dev convenience): per-project secrets file
echo 'postgresql://mtix_sync@hub.example.com:5432/mtix_hub?sslmode=verify-full' > .mtix/secrets
chmod 0600 .mtix/secrets
# .mtix/secrets is auto-gitignored by 'mtix sync init'
```

**Never commit the DSN to a tracked yaml.** `mtix sync init` scans
`.mtix/config.{yaml,yml,json}` for DSN-shaped keys and **refuses to
proceed** if any are present (fail-closed).

### 4. Initialize the hub (one teammate, once)

From one team machine:

```bash
mtix sync init
```

This runs schema migration (single-flighted via PG advisory lock),
registers your project on the hub (`sync_projects` table with the
project's first_event_hash), and pushes the local history. Subsequent
teammates clone instead — see step 5.

### 5. Clone from the hub (every other teammate)

From each other team machine, in a fresh checkout:

```bash
mtix sync clone
```

Pulls the full event log from the hub and replays it into the local
SQLite. Idempotent — re-running clone after the initial pull is a
no-op (per `applied_events` dedupe).

Clone first runs, on every hub event, the checks `mtix sync pull` runs
(see "Pulled event fails its checks" under Failure modes). If the hub
holds an event that fails them, clone refuses, naming the event, and
writes nothing. On the fresh store, run `mtix sync pull` instead: pull
quarantines that event and applies the rest. On a store that already
holds local sync state, `mtix sync reconcile --discard-local` (typed ticket count at an
interactive terminal) comes first. It deletes local tasks and unpushed
changes, so first run `mtix sync push` and check that `mtix sync status`
shows `pending` 0. It then asks for the ticket count, typed at an interactive
terminal; no flag supplies it, so it cannot run unattended and an agent asks
the user to run it.

### 6. Daily flow

```bash
# Pull teammate work before you start
mtix sync pull

# Make changes via the normal CLI
mtix create "..." && mtix update ... && mtix done ...

# Push your queue to the hub
mtix sync push

# Check status when in doubt about pending items
mtix sync status
```

`mtix sync pull` resumes after the last event it fetched (its sync
clock and event id), saved in the same transaction as each batch, so
events that share a clock are all fetched and an interrupted pull never
skips a batch. `mtix sync clone` sets that position when it completes,
so the first pull after a clone fetches only newer changes.

`mtix sync pull` also recovers **late events**: changes a teammate
pushed after working offline, which carry lower clock values than your
pull cursor. After fetching past its cursor, pull lists the hub events
created since its previous sweep (with a 15-minute overlap, timed by
the hub's clock), notes the ones you do not have, and then applies them
the usual way, oldest first by the sync clock; an older recovered claim
or status change never reverts a newer one. If the regular fetch meets
an edit of a task whose creation arrived late, pull runs the sweep at
once and retries the fetch one time, so the creation applies first.
The first pull after upgrading compares the full hub history once and
prints how many late events it recovered; if a pull stops part-way, the
next one continues where it stopped. `mtix sync status` shows `last
sweep`. In the common case (nothing missing, at most `--limit` changes
since the last sweep) the sweep costs one extra hub query per pull,
more for a larger window or recovered changes, and it has no timer of
its own. After
upgrading, the hub owner runs `mtix sync init` once with the owner's
DSN to add the hub indexes the sweep and the pull cursor read by. It
runs in one transaction, and pushes and pulls both wait until it
finishes (longer on a large hub, while it builds the indexes), so run it
when a pause in sync is acceptable. Until then pulls fetch the same events,
only slower: each page the sweep lists scans the hub's event table
(once per pull for the usual window, and once per page of the one-time
full comparison, spread across pulls), and so does each pull batch.

### 7. Install the pre-push hook (recommended)

```bash
cp examples/hooks/pre-push .git/hooks/pre-push
chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-push
```

The hook calls `mtix sync push` before `git push`. It honors
`MTIX_SYNC_HOOK=1` (set automatically by the hook script): transient
hub-unreachable errors warn-and-skip rather than blocking the push.

### 8. (Optional) Run the daemon for periodic auto-push

For durability across machine loss, install the daemon as a
systemd / launchd service:

```bash
# Print the systemd unit file (Linux) — operator places it
mtix sync daemon --install | sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/mtix-sync.service
sudo systemctl enable --now mtix-sync
```

The daemon runs `mtix sync pull` on an interval (default 30s) and
keeps the local view fresh. See the [safety-critical
workflow](safety-critical.md) for full guidance on hardening the
service.

