## Conflict handling

When two teammates edit the same field concurrently, the hub
records the contention in `sync_conflicts` and the apply layer
resolves it via Last-Write-Wins (deterministic by lamport →
wall_clock_ts → author_machine_hash).

```bash
# Inspect the contested entries (unresolved only; --all lists every row)
mtix sync conflicts list

# Record a decision for a specific conflict (per-conflict, surgical).
# It changes no node: apply the chosen value with mtix update, then push.
mtix sync conflicts resolve <id> --action keep-local
mtix sync conflicts resolve <id> --action keep-remote

# Whole-project escape hatch (only if many conflicts at once)
mtix sync reconcile --dry-run
mtix sync reconcile --discard-local   # accept hub state: deletes local tasks and unpushed changes; run `mtix sync push` first, pending 0; asks for the ticket count at a terminal
mtix sync reconcile --rename-to NEWPREFIX  # publish locally under a fresh prefix
mtix sync reconcile --import-as PARENT-ID  # graft local nodes under a hub node
```

LWW is deterministic — every CLI ends up at the same final state
regardless of the order in which they pull. Conflicts are recorded
for auditability, not because the system is uncertain.

Claims and status changes (`mtix claim`, `unclaim`, `done`, `cancel`,
`defer`, `reopen` and the other transitions) are resolved per task by
last-writer-wins when they are pulled: every machine shows the status
set by the most recent claim or status change (highest
`lamport_clock`, ties broken by event id), and an older one that
arrives later changes nothing. These contests are resolved silently
and are not listed by `mtix sync conflicts list`. The status agrees
for claims and status changes, which travel as events; two automatic
changes do not, so status can still differ there (a task claimed on
one machine while another adds a dependency that blocks it can end up
blocked on one and in progress on the other, and descendants of a
cascade cancel can differ the same way: they are not cancelled on
other machines, yet a task the cascade unblocked shows unblocked there
although its blocker is not cancelled; when other machines must see
the descendants cancelled, cancel them one at a time, deepest first,
without `--cascade`). Beyond status nothing is guaranteed to agree: the assignee can still differ between machines
when changes arrive out of order (if two agents claim a task between
syncs and the one whose claim lost marks it done before pulling, both
machines show it done, each with its own agent as the assignee). After
`mtix sync pull`, check a claimed task with `mtix show <id>` and stop
if it is no longer in progress under your name.

A deferral travels as a status change, but hub sync does not carry its
wake time (`mtix defer <id> --until <time>`) in 0.5.x. On the other
machines the task is deferred with no wake time
(any wake time it held before is cleared): it is not reopened when the
time passes, and a claim there is not refused. A deferred task with no
wake time is listed by `mtix ready` and can be claimed at once. Every
claim or status change, local or received through sync, clears the
wake time as the task leaves deferred. Set the wake time on the machine
that will pick the task up. A git-tracked `.mtix/tasks.json` does carry
`defer_until`, so a machine that imports it gets the wake time.

**Note:** When two CLIs share `authorID="cli"` (the default), their
concurrent events produce vector clocks that are `Equal()` rather
than `Concurrent()`, so the hub does NOT log a `sync_conflicts` row
even though LWW still resolves the contention. If audit-trail
completeness for same-authorID conflicts matters, set distinct
authorIDs per agent. See `docs/SECURITY-MODEL.md` for the full
tradeoff discussion.

